###Pure
// CONFLUENCE: mapping constructs that exist in this corpus only in isolation, put on one
// class so a single query reaches all of them.
//
// The stacking scoreboard counts how many feature PAIRS co-occur in a passing service, and
// 148 of the 820 are two mapping-level constructs whose mappings never meet anywhere in the
// corpus. Those cannot be closed by writing a query -- only by changing the model so the
// constructs live where one query reaches both. This file is that change, for two of them:
//
//   Otherwise   an embedded block with a fallback set     -- reporting::OtherwiseMapping
//   scope       a block factoring the table prefix out    -- dense::Mapping
//
// Each was alone in its own mapping, so `Otherwise + scope` had never executed. Here they are
// one class mapping, and spread.py generates the query shapes over it, so the pair also meets
// `graph fetch`, `groupBy`, `query filter` and the rest.
//
// A NEW class rather than trading::Trade, which is what the first version used. The corpus
// reader keys a property's column by CLASS rather than by (mapping, class), so mapping Trade
// a second time onto a narrower table collapses its column resolution GLOBALLY -- thirteen
// properties reported missing, and every Trade expectation in the corpus silently resolved
// against the wrong table. A class mapped once cannot collide with anything.
Class conf::ConfTrade
{
   tradeId: String[1];
   notional: Float[0..1];
   status: String[0..1];
}

// The relationship is an ASSOCIATION, not a property declared on the class. The reader
// registers an embedded mapping context for an association END; a directly-declared property
// falls through to plain column mapping, and the embedded block's CPTY_*_INLINE columns then
// overwrite counterparty::Counterparty's global column map -- the same class-collision that
// made the first version rewrite Trade's. 56-otherwise-store.pure reaches its embedded block
// through an association for the same reason, which is why it has never had this problem.
Association conf::ConfTradeCounterparty
{
   confTrade: conf::ConfTrade[0..*];
   counterparty: counterparty::Counterparty[0..1];
}

###Mapping
Mapping conf::Confluence
(
    // The fallback target. `Otherwise` names a ROOT set implementation of the property's
    // type, so one has to exist for counterparty::Counterparty in this mapping.
    counterparty::Counterparty[confCptyRoot]: Relational {
        ~primaryKey ([store::DB] COUNTERPARTY.COUNTERPARTY_ID)
        ~mainTable [store::DB] COUNTERPARTY
        counterpartyId: [store::DB] COUNTERPARTY.COUNTERPARTY_ID,
        legalName: [store::DB] COUNTERPARTY.LEGAL_NAME,
        lei: [store::DB] COUNTERPARTY.LEI
    }

    conf::ConfTrade: Relational {
        ~primaryKey ([store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.TRADE_ID)
        ~mainTable [store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL
        tradeId: [store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.TRADE_ID,
        // The scope block: the table prefix factored out of the properties inside it. The
        // same columns either way -- what differs is the SPELLING, and a spelling used in
        // only one mapping is a spelling nothing had ever combined with anything.
        scope([store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL)
        (
            notional: NOTIONAL,
            status: STATUS
        ),
        // The embedded block with its fallback. When CPTY_ID_INLINE is present the inline
        // columns answer; when it is not, the join to the root set does.
        counterparty
        (
            ~primaryKey ([store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.CPTY_ID_INLINE)
            counterpartyId: [store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.CPTY_ID_INLINE,
            legalName: [store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.CPTY_NAME_INLINE,
            lei: [store::DB] TRADE_FLAT_PARTIAL.CPTY_LEI_INLINE
        ) Otherwise ([confCptyRoot]: [store::DB] @TradeFlatPartial_Counterparty)
    }
)

###Runtime
Runtime conf::ConfluenceRT
{
    mappings: [ conf::Confluence ];
    connections: [ store::DB: [ environment: store::Conn ] ];
}
